In article <2384@zipeecs.umich.edu> huggins@dip.eecs.umich.edu (James K. Huggins) writes:
>A question on colors and a question on alignment
>   (perhaps the same question?  I dunno ... that's why I'm asking):
> 
>(Oh, ... I'm playing on a PC with a color monitor)
> 
>It seems that some of the monsters are drawn with different
>colors than other monsters.  I'm assuming that this is significant,
>since it doesn't appear to be random behavior.  What's the 
>significance?

The main significance is that each monster or object is consistently
drawn in a specific color.  If there is a color associated with a
given monster or item (e.g. red molds, blue jellies, Grey (sic.)
Elves, worthless pieces of green glass, etc.), that is refelcted on
the screen (black items, e.g. black caps or dragons, are drawn in dark
gray); otherwise, a decision is made more or less arbitrarily.  (I
assume 'color' is coded into the source descriptions of monsters,
since when there is a logical color for a monster, it is used (e.g.
leprechauns are green).)

The main significance is that once you learn which colors go with
which monsters, you have to use the / key less often.  (e.g. when you
see an 'F', if it's green, red, or brown, you may want to use a
missile weapon; if it's yellow or violet, you can whale away at will.
And when you see a red 'D', you know which ring to put on PDQ.)

> 
>Also -- I've seen lots of hints here to not kill monsters of your
>own alignment.  How do you tell if a monster is of your alignment?
>(Does that have anything to do with the colors?)

Usually not.  White unicorns (lawful) are white on-screen; gray and
black unicorns (neutral and chaotic, respectively) are both gray, so
you have to use the old '/' key.

They are relevant in that when you learn certain color associations,
you know what monsters are more likely to be peaceful, which may mean
they're co-aligned.  For example, if you are lawful, then when you see
a red or green 'h' (dwarfs and hobbits, respectively), you may not
want to attack, since these beasties are usually lawful.  Brown
aitches are bugbears; fire at will.  Also, white d's and f's are dogs
and cats, respectively; don't eat these.  You can eat any other color
of 'f' or 'd' (of course, you have to turn it into a '%' first).

This is convenient, but not essential.  If you have the 'confirm'
option on, you will be asked for confirmation before attacking any
peaceful animal.  The sure way to know an animal's alignment is to use
a stethescope or a wand of probing.

